{
  "id": "Compound::DB00891 - Gene::1559",
  "verbalization": "Sulfapyridine, sulfamoxole, and silver sulfadiazine are all structurally related to sulfonamides, a class of antibiotics that share a common core sulfonamide ring. These compounds are all predicted to bind to Cyp2c9, a cytochrome P450 enzyme involved in drug metabolism. The evidence suggests that Sulfapyridine, through structural similarity to sulfonamide antibiotics, may interact with Cyp2c9, potentially inhibiting its activity. This mechanism is supported by the shared pathway of these compounds leading to sulfadimethoxine, which is known to bind to Cyp2c9. The low common ancestors of these compounds all point to the sulfonamide class, reinforcing the hypothesis that Sulfapyridine may act via a sulfonamide-like mechanism on Cyp2c9. The high scores in the final scores indicate a strong, consistent relationship across multiple pathways, suggesting a plausible and biologically relevant interaction.",
